      "text": "Given the spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left( 3, \\frac{5 \\pi}{12}, 0 \\right)$, we need to convert them to rectangular coordinates $(x, y, z)$.\n\nFirst, recall the relationships between spherical and rectangular coordinates:\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\rho = 3, \\quad \\theta = \\frac{5 \\pi}{12}, \\quad \\phi = 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\phi = 0\\), \\(\\sin \\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = 1\\). Using \\(\\phi = 0\\), the equations simplify to:\n\\[\nx = 3 \\sin(0) \\cos\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 3 \\sin(0) \\sin\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 3 \\cos(0)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin(0) = 0\\) and \\(\\cos(0) = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\cos\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}\\right) = 0\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}\\right) = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the rectangular coordinates are:\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (0, 0, 3)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0, 3)}\n\\]",
